agraph numbered R13 in p4ng/sec-catalog.tex; see PREREG-war-machine §1b\n\n! conclusion: Genuine planning (R15) requires evaluating policies G(π) over a horizon and separating timescales; a single rollout step demonstrates the machinery, not the depth. Depth-1 is reactive control wearing planning's clothes.\n\n  + context: R15 asks for hierarchical / temporal depth — the agent evaluates policies over a horizon of several ticks (not just the next action), and represents goals that span many ticks via a slow loop that sets priors for a fast loop.\n\n  + IF:\n    You want the agent to plan — to prefer an action whose payoff is two or three ticks out over a greedy one-step win, and to pursue goals (like accumulating capability) that no single tick can achieve.\n\n  + HOWEVER:\n    A rollout that looks ONE step ahead — even one that passes a \"2-step-beats-greedy\" witness — demonstrates the MACHINERY (multi-step G(π) is computable) but not DEPTH. The horizon is still shallow, and without a temporal HIERARCHY (a slow loop shaping a fast one) the agent cannot represent a goal whose satisfaction is spread across many ticks. It reacts one step at a time and calls it planning.\n\n  + THEN:\n    Extend G(π) evaluation to a horizon H > 1 with a temporal discount, AND introduce a two-timescale hierarchy: a slow (strategic) loop that updates priors/preferences and a fast (tactical) loop that acts against them. The slow loop is where accumulated learning (R12) and prior-preferences (R19) live; the fast loop is the per-tick act-gate. Couple the two so the fast loop's realized outcomes update the slow loop's priors.\n\n  + BECAUSE:\n    Active inference plans by minimizing expected free energy over a POLICY (a temporally-extended sequence), not over the next action alone. A depth-1 evaluation cannot distinguish a locally-optimal dead-end from a step toward a distant goal. Temporal hierarchy is also the natural home for the capability curriculum (\"work descends into capability\") —