d ellipses are rejected, and rung 1 also requires the source pattern id to occur in the record. If the supplied rung-1 hits do not warrant an edge, run rung 2. If neither warrants an edge, return Absence naming both. You are an editor: you may write @how and @see-also. An @why item is a PROPOSAL only; the runner records its attestation but does not write its directive.\n\nREADME-flexiarg.md §5a (verbatim):\n| directive | direction | meaning | who writes it |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `@why <id> [<id> …]` | toward the general | the authority this pattern rests on — the strategy it is an instance of | the pattern's own author |\n| `@how <id> [<id> …]` | toward the specific | the named methods by which this pattern is carried out | an editor, later |\n| `@see-also <id> [<id> …]` | sideways | a peer technique; no claim of authority in either direction | either |\nRunner-supplied rung-1 exact-occurrence hits (cite these verbatim; do not invent others):\n[]\n\nAttestation schema:\n{:edge {:from :pattern-id\n        :to :pattern-id\n        :kind #{:why :how :see-also}}\n :by :agent-id\n :at :date\n :read [:pattern-id]\n :cited :nonblank-string\n :evidence [{:id :evidence-id\n             :via #{:tag :text}\n             :query :nonblank-string\n             :excerpt :nonblank-string}]\n :rung #{1 2}\n :state #{:proposed [:attested-by :agent-id] :refused}\n :reason :optional-nonblank-string}\n\nSection README:\n(No section README exists.)\nSection pattern ids:\n[\"aif/admissibility\" \"aif/belief-aware-risk-term\" \"aif/belief-state-operational-hypotheses\" \"aif/candidate-pattern-action-space\" \"aif/currency-before-merge\" \"aif/declare-the-conditioning\" \"aif/decomposed-prediction-noise\" \"aif/evidence-precision-registry\" \"aif/expected-free-energy-scorecard\" \"aif/experimental-comparison-of-EFE-variants\" \"aif/free-energy-as-tick-scalar\" \"aif/grounded-actuation-not-reobservation\" \"aif/hierarchical-and-temporal-depth\" \"aif/hierarchical-budget-aware-action-selection\" \"aif/interoceptive-tripwires\" \"aif/measurement-window-hygiene\" \"aif/niche-construction\" \"aif/no-self-certification\" \"aif/off-continuity-null-discriminates\" \"aif/placeholder-is-load-bearing\" \"aif/policy-precision-commitment-temperature\" \"aif/posterior-variance-as-epistemic-value\" \"aif/predictive-coding-belief-update\" \"aif/predictive-entropy-as-ambiguity\" \"aif/scheduled-observer-entrypoint\" \"aif/shared-kernel-predictive-forward-model\" \"aif/status-gated-belief-update\" \"aif/structure-learning-by-model-reduction\" \"aif/structured-observation-vector\" \"aif/temporal-depth-beyond-greedy\" \"aif/term-to-channel-traceability\" \"aif/two-layer-calibration\" \"aif/valuation-reads-the-paperwork\"]\n\nSelected pattern text:\n@flexiarg aif/off-continuity-null-discriminates\n@title An Off-Continuity Null Discriminates (No Move Needed When the Models Disagree at the Begin)\n@sigils [∅/≠]\n@keywords null measurement, model discrimination, calibration, continuity point, off-continuity, dual prediction, state-sensitive vs state-blind, forward model, clean-target wall, no-move prediction, settled window\n@audience anyone calibrating competing forward models against realised outcomes when moving the system is scarce\n@tone practical\n@style pattern\n@why war-room/wr-9-bites-empirical-or-logical   ;; editorial, claude-13 2026-08-23: retrospective sense-making, NOT a causal\n;; claim -- WR-19 did not produce this pattern. See README-flexiarg 5a.\n\n@see-also aif/experimental-comparison-of-EFE-variants\n! conclusion: A null — the system did not move — is usually written off as uninformative for model selection. It is the opposite when the begin sits OFF the continuity point where the competing models agree. There, a state-sensitive model predicts the no-move while a state-blind (constant) model predicts a move that never happens, so a clean null separates them by the full off-continuity gap. When a clean-target wall has exhausted the counter-moving cases, an off-continuity null is the cheapest discriminating evidence you can buy: discrimination comes from the models DISAGREEING at the begin, not from the field MOVING.\n\n  + context: You calibrate a forward model by recording predicted/realised pairs, logging a DUAL\n    prediction — a state-sensitive estimate (scaled by the target's live state) beside a state-blind\n    constant. The \"continuity point\" is the state where the two coincide (e.g. an open-hole-count of 3\n    where scaled == constant). A \"null\" pair is one where the system does not move (no counter-moving\n    hole was available; the act was an honest content-advance that changed no counted quantity).\n\n  + IF:\n      You want a pair that DISCRIMINATES the two forward models, but the only pairs you can fly are\n      nulls — the clean-target wall has run out of targets whose counted quantity you can actually move.\n\n  + HOWEVER:\n      Conventional reading says a null carries no signal — no Δ to compare. And a null AT the\n      continuity point genuinely is model-indifferent: both models predict the same value, so matching\n      reality distinguishes nothing. (Witness live-957a4836: a clean dynamics pair, a real MOVE, yet\n      scaled == constant at the continuity point — 0x discrimination from a clean move.)\n\n  + THEN:\n      Pick an OFF-continuity begin — a below-cap target where the two models predict DIFFERENT values\n      at the current state. Fly a tight null there (a content-advance moving no counted hole; settled\n      window, two scans agreeing within ε). The realised lands on the state-sensitive prediction and\n      misses the constant by exactly the off-continuity gap. The null IS the discriminating\n      measurement; the state-blind model's error is the state it cannot see.\n\n  + BECAUSE:\n      A move tests a magnitude prediction; a no-move tests a no-move prediction. Both are predictions\n      and both can be wrong. Discrimination is a property of the models disagreeing at the begin, not\n      of the field changing — so a null off the continuity point is fully informative, while a clean\n      move on the continuity point is not. (Caught live: live-df706c45, an open-hole-count-4\n      off-continuity null — scaled error 7.65e-5 vs constant error 0.0396, ~517x discrimination from a\n      NULL, where the continuity-point witness got 0x from a clean MOVE. The two are the canonical\n      contrast cases.)\n\n  + how-to-apply: When the wall exhausts counter-moving holes, do not conclude \"no measurable pairs\n    remain.\" Find a below-cap target where the candidate models disagree now (off the continuity\n    point), fly a tight settled null on it, and read the dual prediction — the state-blind model's\n    error is the discrimination you needed. Mask-in still requires the null be CLEAN: settled, two\n    distinct-as-of scans agreeing within ε ([[measurement-window-hygiene]]); a confounded null\n    discriminates nothing. This is the L1 dynamics layer earning its keep — discriminating models\n    without ever weighing the action against value ([[two-layer-calibration]]).\n",
