ce anyone calibrating a value/forward model against a field the model helps compose\n@tone architectural\n@style pattern\n@holds-at R12   ;; pegged 2026-08-30 (claude-15): the catalogue paragraph numbered R12 in p4ng/sec-catalog.tex; see PREREG-war-machine §1b\n\n! conclusion: When you calibrate a model whose own outputs compose the field it is scored against, split the calibration into TWO layers and never let one stand in for the other. L1 — dynamics/consistency (does the field respond to measured state?): cheap, accrues every cycle, and is NEVER value evidence. L2 — outcome-vs-prediction (did real functioning result?): scarce, witnessed, gated, and is the ONLY value clearance.\n\n  + context: You are calibrating a forward/value model against a live field, and the field's value\n    function is computed from the same effects the model predicts with (`prior == value` risk).\n\n  + IF:\n      Predicted-vs-realised pairs are your evidence that the model is \"calibrated\".\n\n  + HOWEVER:\n      On such a field the comparison is largely self-referential: it tests dynamics-consistency\n      (did the world move as the model's state-response said?), NOT whether the model's VALUE is\n      right — because the realised IS the model's own output. A model can pass this layer forever\n      while being worthless at predicting real outcomes. Worse, fixing the obvious circularity\n      (making predict-effects state-sensitive) RELOCATES it rather than removing it: state-blindness\n      breaks, but the error *magnitude* stays model-priced.\n\n  + THEN:\n      Name and keep both layers explicitly. **L1 = G-vs-G dynamics**: cheap, every cycle, labelled\n      never-value-evidence; it earns its keep as a consistency/sanity layer. **L2 =\n      outcome-vs-prediction** against WITNESSED outcomes the model did not produce — closure-folds,\n      three-witness certificates, attested real functioning — scarce, gated behind the reward\n      discipline. Only L2 clears value. Forbid L1's cheap pass from being reported as L2 clearance.\n\n  + BECAUSE:\n      The only thing that escapes `prior == value` is a referent the model had no hand in. L1's\n      referent (the field) is the model's own output, so L1 can only ever measure internal\n      consistency. L2's referent (a witnessed outcome) is external, so it is the only layer that can\n      be WRONG about value — which is exactly why it is the gate, not an enhancement.\n\n  + canonical-instance:\n      FutonZero G-SIM (WM-pilot, 2026-06-11): L1 dynamics live — the state-sensitive model is 2.55×\n      better than the constant counterfactual at predicting no-move — while L2 stays gated behind\n      pudding-G1's binding of the arrow-witness to witnessed affect/outcome. The split was forced by\n      discovering the calibration was self-certifying (`[[aif/no-self-certification]]`).\n",
