orse: every firing writes a green \"it ran\" row, so the trace manufactures the appearance of operation and hides the inertia. Scheduled-execution-*ready* is mistaken for scheduled-execution-*doing*.\n\n  + THEN:\n    Install the scheduled entrypoint, BUT gate \"R10 = ✓\" on evidence that a tick changed observable substrate state (a dial moved, a hole closed) — not merely that the entrypoint fired. When the loop can only no-op, DISABLE the cron rather than let it paint green; re-enable it precisely when a tick can do real work (i.e. when R16's grounded actuation can move a dial). Add a liveness assertion: N consecutive scheduled runs with zero state-delta FLAGS, it does not PASS.\n\n  + BECAUSE:\n    Liveness is a property of state change, not of the scheduler firing. A cron over a sealed loop is a clock ticking in an empty room. The honest signal of \"live\" is a substrate delta attributable to a scheduled tick; absent that, a green cadence is the most convincing possible disguise for a dead loop.\n\n  + NEXT-STEPS:\n    - DONE: the scheduled entrypoint exists (`futon2 -M:wm-scheduled`, hourly). The apparatus is real.\n    - DONE (2026-07-06): DISABLED the hourly cron — cached no-op runs sufficed; re-enable when the loop does real work. Evidence: futon2/holes/aif-wiring-actuator.html status panel.\n    - Couple R10 to R16: a scheduled tick counts as \"live\" only if it can (and does) move the substrate dial via grounded actuation.\n    - Add the liveness assertion to the tick harness: `zero-delta-over-N` → flag, with the run count surfaced (never silently green).\n    - Distinguish in the trace schema: `:tick/fired?` (the scheduler ran) vs `:tick/moved-substrate?` (a dial changed). R10 reads the second, not the first.\n",
