 strategic loop that chooses which task\n    to work on and updates priors and preferences slowly, and a tactical loop that constructs and\n    executes a cascade against the chosen target quickly. (R13 names the two loops; R11 is the\n    orthogonal resource hierarchy over a shared budget. This pattern is the coupling between the\n    two timescales.)\n\n  + IF:\n      A fast tactical loop acts inside a slower strategic loop.\n\n  + HOWEVER:\n      Merely giving both levels a scalar score does not create a hierarchy. A second use of the\n      same score — the commitment dial doing duty as a temporal discount, a tactical outcome read\n      as if it were strategic evidence — collapses the levels back into one, and nothing in the\n      record can then say which level learned what.\n\n  + THEN:\n      Make the coupling explicit, in three named seams: (1) strategic selection fixes the tactical\n      target — the tactical loop does not choose what to work on; (2) witnessed tactical outcomes\n      update the next strategic calibration state — an outcome enters the slow level only through\n      a witness the fast level did not manufacture; (3) longer policies use a named temporal\n      discount of their own, rather than overloading the commitment dial (R14) to stand in for\n      time preference.\n\n  + BECAUSE:\n      Hierarchy is a relationship between levels and timescales, not a second use of the same\n      score. Each level has its own quantities; the invariant to preserve is that no quantity does\n      duty at two levels at once.\n      + evidence: the opt-in two-tick campaign records both levels separately, rejects unwitnessed\n        learning, folds a grounded fast outcome into a Beta update of the slow state, and uses that\n        state to parameterise the next fast rollout. Campaign S (2026-08-21): Tick A's independently\n        approved and grounded outcome advanced the slow posterior from Beta(1,1) to Beta(2,1),\n        changed the slow mode from exploration to consolidation, and parameterised Tick B under\n        that state. This is evidence for the explicit coupling, not for a general nested\n        generative model.\n      + COUNTERFACTUAL: a run in which a tactical outcome changed the strategic state without a\n        witness, or in which the commitment dial and the temporal discount are one number, would\n        show the coupling is not explicit.\n",
