al consistency, prediction error, confound\n@audience anyone measuring an action's effect on a drifting, settling system\n@tone practical\n@style pattern\n\n! conclusion: To measure an action's effect on a system that drifts and settles over time, capture the BASELINE tight — right before the action, not before the preparatory work — and read the EFFECT settled — after the state stabilizes (≥2 consecutive reads agree within ε) — and EXCLUDE any pair where drift or transience confounds the window. A before/after number is only as good as the window it brackets.\n\n  + context: You measure `effect = after − before` around an action, in a system whose state\n    propagates asynchronously (scans, caches, eventual consistency, a live field other agents touch).\n\n  + IF:\n      You record a predicted/realised (or before/after) pair to score an action's effect on such a\n      system.\n\n  + HOWEVER:\n      Two window failures inflate or fabricate the number, and both look clean:\n      (a) STALE BASELINE — you captured \"before\" *early* (e.g. before minting the artifact the action\n      commits), and the system drifted in between; the measured Δ is mostly that drift, not the action.\n      (b) TRANSIENT READBACK — you read \"after\" at the first post-action sample, catching an in-flight\n      spike that decays to baseline; the measured Δ is a scan-timing artifact.\n\n  + THEN:\n      Bracket the window tightly. BEFORE: capture the baseline immediately prior to the field-visible\n      action — do the preparatory work first, *then* baseline, *then* commit. AFTER: read at the\n      settled state — require ≥2 consecutive reads agreeing within ε; tag `:settled` vs `:transient`\n      and exclude transient. And if the system drifted independently inside the window, DROP the pair\n      (untagged / proposal-mode) rather than attribute the drift to the action.\n\n  + BECAUSE:\n      A measurement's validity lives in its window, not its arithmetic. The durable effect is what