pability-portfolio breadth, count of available action branches, action-class diversity). These approximate but don't actually compute the AIF quantity.\n\n  + IF:\n    You want the ambiguity term to be the *true predictive entropy* of the observation distribution under each candidate. Candidates that produce sharper predictions (lower predictive variance) get lower ambiguity; candidates that produce diffuse predictions (high predictive variance, near-threshold predictions, etc.) get higher ambiguity. The agent prefers ambiguity-reducing candidates because they're information-gaining.\n\n  + HOWEVER:\n    Substituting a heuristic for the actual entropy makes the agent's \"epistemic\" preferences correlated-with-but-not-equal-to information theory. The agent may prefer actions that have many sub-options but produce nothing informative; or avoid actions that look unambiguous in heuristic terms but produce truly precise observations. Heuristic-based epistemic terms are common but not AIF-canonical.\n\n  + THEN:\n    For a candidate action producing predicted observation distribution `o ∼ N(μ, Σ)`, the differential entropy of `o` is:\n      `H[o] = 0.5 × log((2πe)^k × det(Σ))`\n    where k is the dimension of the observation vector. For diagonal-covariance (independent channels), `det(Σ) = Π_k σ²_k`. For comparing across candidates, the additive constants (`0.5 × k × log(2πe)`) drop out, leaving:\n      `ambiguity-comparable(candidate) = 0.5 × log(Π_k σ²_k)`\n    In two dimensions (D, A): `ambiguity-comparable = 0.5 × log(σ²_D × σ²_A)`.\n\n  + BECAUSE:\n    Predictive entropy is the AIF-canonical epistemic quantity. The diagonal-Gaussian simplification is tractable and matches the diagonal-covariance assumption that downstream code (mode-quadrant integration; visualisation) already uses. Dropping constants preserves comparability across candidates while avoiding spurious absolute-entropy precision — a value of `−4.3` in dropped-constants form is comparable to `−4.4` for 