iances rank equal under bias-only risk — which is wrong: the high-variance candidate carries higher operational risk of missing preference even though it has the same expected outcome.\n\n  + THEN:\n    Compute the risk term per institution (or per state-instance) as:\n      `risk[i] = (mean-D[i] - prior-D)² + (mean-A[i] - prior-A)² + var-D[i] + var-A[i]`\n    Both the squared-deviation (bias²) and the predictive variance contribute additively to the per-instance risk. Average over instances in the band for the band-level risk-term. Configurable priors per opts.\n\n  + BECAUSE:\n    Under a Gaussian observation likelihood, the expected squared deviation from preference is `bias² + variance` (the variance-bias decomposition). Including both terms makes the risk match the canonical pragmatic value in AIF. Including only bias is a common implementation shortcut that produces a non-Bayesian agent.\n\n  + NEXT-STEPS:\n    - Confirm your forward model returns predicted variance, not just predicted mean. If only mean is available, you can only compute the bias half — and the agent is not properly belief-aware in its risk scoring.\n    - Read predicted (mean, variance) per state-instance from the forward-model output; compose the per-instance risk as `Σ ((mean_k - prior_k)² + var_k)` over coordinates k.\n    - Average over instances in the relevant scope (band, cohort, agent's responsibility area). Comparable across candidates if the scope is fixed.\n    - Document the priors: which coordinate has which preference value, why, where it lives in config. Priors should be configurable per opts, not hardcoded.\n    - DONE (UKRN v3 HO-05, 2026-05-14): risk-term-v3 implemented at notebooks/ukrn_v3_efe.clj:42-58. Uses both (D, A) coordinates; reads predicted variance from HO-04's forward model; PRIOR-D = PRIOR-A = 0.85 with opts override. Test risk-term-v3-has-prior-and-variance-properties verifies the variance contribution is additive. Closes the materially-incomplete-policy-score