nly for instances the agent is actually observing this tick. Paused, truncated, dropped, or otherwise out-of-loop instances retain their prior belief unchanged.\n\n  + context: When an AIF agent operates over multiple instances (institutions, sessions, processes, conversation threads), each instance has an engagement status that determines whether the agent receives meaningful observations from it. The naive update-everything-every-tick policy is operationally wrong when some instances are not contributing fresh information.\n\n  + IF:\n    You want the belief evolution to faithfully reflect what the agent actually saw — not what it would have seen under unconditional observation. You want paused or truncated instances to retain their last-known belief as a marker for \"the agent stopped observing here at this prior\", rather than drifting under noise or stale dynamics.\n\n  + HOWEVER:\n    A blanket \"update every instance every tick\" policy will:\n      - Mistake rollback dynamics (e.g. paused-status state rollback) for genuine observations, eroding the agent's stored belief about non-observed instances.\n      - Cause truncated instances' beliefs to drift toward their frozen-but-decaying state, producing post-truncation noise in trace records.\n      - Make it hard to read trace records, because every instance moves every tick regardless of whether it should.\n\n  + THEN:\n    Gate the belief update on instance status, distinguishing *engagement status* (does the agent act on the instance this tick) from *observation availability* (does the agent see the instance's state this tick):\n      - For status :active — agent is engaging *and* observing. Apply update-mu using the post-step state.\n      - For status :paused — agent is *not engaging* but *is still observing* (the dynamics still computes the instance's (D, A) state; only the engagement-side effects are rolled back). Apply update-mu using the post-step (D, A) as observation.\n      - For status :truncated 